  "Number": 11,
  "Question": "Real numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) satisfy the equation \\[x^2+y^2=10x-6y-34.\\] What is \\(x+y?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\( 1 \\)",
    "B": "\\( 2 \\)",
    "C": "\\( 3 \\)",
    "D": "\\( 6 \\)",
    "E": "\\( 8 \\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The linear terms suggest completing a square for each variable. Rearrange the equation and add \\(25+9=34\\) to both sides:\n\\[\nx^2-10x+y^2+6y=-34,\n\\]\n\\[\n(x-5)^2+(y+3)^2=0.\n\\]\nBecause \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are real, both squares are nonnegative. Their sum can be zero only if both are zero. Hence \\(x=5\\) and \\(y=-3\\). These values give \\(34\\) on both sides of the original equation, and\n\\[\nx+y=5-3=\\boxed{2},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
